  <!-- SECTION 2: PROBE SIGNALS & CYTOGENETIC KEY -->
  <section id="fish-probe-key" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="fish-key-title">
    <div class="fish-panel-header-wrap">
      <div>
        <h2 id="fish-key-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">Probe Signals &amp; Cytogenetic Key</h2>
        <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">The 9 distinct tandem repeat and chromosome-specific probes utilized in the Kato et al. multicolor FISH cocktail.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="fish-probe-grid">
      <!-- 1: Knob 180-bp -->
      <article class="fish-probe-card">
        <div class="fish-probe-badge-row">
          <span class="fish-color-swatch swatch-blue" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fish-probe-tag">Knob 180-bp Repeat</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Knob 180-bp \(Blue\)</h3>
        <p>Major tandem repeat element marking knob positions across chromosome arms; exhibits extensive presence/absence polymorphism among lines.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- 2: 5S rDNA -->
      <article class="fish-probe-card">
        <div class="fish-probe-badge-row">
          <span class="fish-color-swatch swatch-yellow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fish-probe-tag">5S 2-3-3 Clone</span>
        </div>
        <h3>5S rDNA \(Yellow &bull; 2L\)</h3>
        <p>Specific marker hybridizing to the 5S ribosomal RNA gene cluster located on the long arm of Chromosome 2 \(2L\).</p>
      </article>

      <!-- 3: NOR-173 -->
      <article class="fish-probe-card">
        <div class="fish-probe-badge-row">
          <span class="fish-color-swatch swatch-green" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fish-probe-tag">NOR-173 Clone</span>
        </div>
        <h3>NOR-173 \(Green &bull; 6S\)</h3>
        <p>Nucleolus organizer region \(45S rDNA\) probe marking the secondary constriction on the short arm of Chromosome 6 \(6S\).</p>
      </article>

      <!-- 4: CentC -->
      <article class="fish-probe-card">
        <div class="fish-probe-badge-row">
          <span class="fish-color-swatch swatch-green" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fish-probe-tag">CentC Satellite</span>
        </div>
        <h3>CentC \(Green\)</h3>
        <p>Conserved centromeric satellite repeat hybridizing to functional centromeres across all maize chromosomes.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- 5: Subtelomeric 4-12-1 -->
      <article class="fish-probe-card">
        <div class="fish-probe-badge-row">
          <span class="fish-color-swatch swatch-green" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fish-probe-tag">Subtelomere 4-12-1</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Subtelomeric 4-12-1 \(Green\)</h3>
        <p>Repetitive clone marking specific chromosome ends and subtelomeric domains across the maize complement.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- 6: Cent4 -->
      <article class="fish-probe-card">
        <div class="fish-probe-badge-row">
          <span class="fish-color-swatch swatch-red" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fish-probe-tag">Cent4 Satellite</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Cent4 \(Red &bull; 4C\)</h3>
        <p>Chromosome 4-specific centromeric satellite repeat that uniquely and unambiguously identifies Chromosome 4.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- 7: Microsatellite 1-26-2 -->
      <article class="fish-probe-card">
        <div class="fish-probe-badge-row">
          <span class="fish-color-swatch swatch-red" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fish-probe-tag">Microsatellite 1-26-2</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Microsatellite 1-26-2 \(Red\)</h3>
        <p>Tandem microsatellite repeat clone providing diagnostic chromosomal position signals on somatic spreads.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- 8: pMTY9ER Telomere -->
      <article class="fish-probe-card">
        <div class="fish-probe-badge-row">
          <span class="fish-color-swatch swatch-red" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fish-probe-tag">pMTY9ER Telomere</span>
        </div>
        <h3>pMTY9ER Telomeric \(Red\)</h3>
        <p>Telomere-associated sequence identifying chromosome termini across somatic metaphase spreads.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- 9: TR-1 Repeat -->
      <article class="fish-probe-card">
        <div class="fish-probe-badge-row">
          <span class="fish-color-swatch swatch-white" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fish-probe-tag">TR-1 Repeat</span>
        </div>
        <h3>TR-1 Repeat \(White / Silver\)</h3>
        <p>350-bp tandem repeat element found in a subset of maize knobs, frequently co-occurring with or distinct from Knob 180-bp repeats.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3: METHODOLOGY & TECHNOLOGY -->
  <section id="fish-methodology" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="fish-methodology-title">
    <div class="fish-panel-header-wrap">
      <div>
        <h2 id="fish-methodology-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">Cytogenetic Methodology &amp; Chromosome Identification</h2>
        <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">How multi-target FISH revolutionized maize chromosome karyotyping in somatic root-tip metaphases.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="fish-method-grid">
      <article class="fish-method-card">
        <h3>One-Step Somatic Chromosome Identification</h3>
        <p>
          Classical maize cytogenetics relied heavily on pachytene spreads in meiotic microsporocytes. The Kato et al. FISH cocktail allowed for the first time rapid, simultaneous identification of all 10 somatic mitotic chromosome pairs in standard root-tip squashes, enabling high-throughput screening of genetic stocks, translocations, and aneuploids.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="fish-method-card">
        <h3>Structural Variation &amp; Heterochromatic Knob Polymorphism</h3>
        <p>
          The comparative atlas across 14 inbred lines reveals extensive natural variation in knob size, repeat composition \(Knob180 vs TR-1\), and chromosomal locations, providing physical cytogenetic validation for sequence-based pan-genome assemblies and structural variation studies.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="fish-method-card">
        <h3>Biological Stocks &amp; Historical Provenance</h3>
        <p>
          The B73 and Mo17 stocks utilized in these landmark cytogenetic experiments were obtained by Dr. James Birchler directly from Dr. Edward Coe, ensuring direct genetic continuity with foundational MaizeGDB genetic stocks.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4: KEY PUBLICATIONS & ABSTRACT PREVIEWS -->
  <section id="fish-publications" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="fish-pub-title">
    <div class="fish-panel-header-wrap">
      <div>
        <h2 id="fish-pub-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">Key Publications &amp; Abstract Previews</h2>
        <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">Primary literature establishing somatic chromosome painting and FISH karyotyping in maize.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="fish-publications-grid">
      <!-- Publication 1: Kato et al. 2004 PNAS -->
      <article class="fish-pub-card">
        <div class="fish-pub-meta-top">
          <span class="fish-pub-journal-badge">Proc Natl Acad Sci USA &bull; 2004</span>
          <span class="fish-pub-doi">DOI&#58; 10.1073/pnas.0403659101</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="fish-pub-title">Chromosome painting using repetitive DNA sequences as probes for somatic chromosome identification in maize</h3>
        <p class="fish-pub-authors">Akio Kato, Jonathan C. Lamb, James A. Birchler</p>
        <p class="fish-pub-citation"><em>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A.</em> 2004 Sep 14; 101\(37\)&#58;13554&ndash;13559.</p>
        
        <div class="fish-pub-abstract-wrap">
          <h4 class="fish-pub-abstract-heading">Abstract</h4>
          <p class="fish-pub-abstract-text">A chromosome identification system using fluorescence in situ hybridization \(FISH\) with repetitive DNA sequences as probes was developed for somatic chromosomes of maize. Probes for the 180-bp knob repeat, TR-1 knob repeat, CentC \(centromere satellite\), Cent4 \(Chromosome 4 centromere\), NOR \(nucleolus organizer region\), 5S ribosomal DNA, subtelomeric 4-12-1 clone, microsatellite 1-26-2, and telomere repeat were labeled with different fluorochromes. By using a single hybridization with a mixture of these probes, every somatic chromosome of maize can be identified simultaneously in metaphase spreads. The karyotypes of 14 inbred lines were analyzed, demonstrating that each line possesses a distinctive FISH karyotype that reflects its specific repetitive sequence composition.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="fish-pub-actions">
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0403659101" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full Text on PNAS &#8599;</a>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="/data_center/reference?id=974526">MaizeGDB Reference Record &#8599;</a>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-quiet" href="/person?id=17766">Dr. Akio Kato Profile</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

