  <!-- SECTION 6: FAQS & GUIDELINES -->
  <section id="fish-faqs" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="fish-faqs-title">
    <div class="fish-panel-header-wrap">
      <div>
        <h2 id="fish-faqs-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">Frequently Asked Questions &amp; Guidelines</h2>
        <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">Common questions regarding probe availability, cytogenetic maps, and stock provenance.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="fish-faq-list">
      <details class="fish-faq-item">
        <summary><h3>How do somatic FISH karyotypes relate to physical genome assemblies?</h3></summary>
        <div class="fish-faq-body">
          <p>
            Somatic FISH karyotypes provide independent physical verification of chromosome structure, centromere positions, and large heterochromatic knob domains. They serve as physical ground truth when scaffolding sequence assemblies and characterizing structural chromosomal rearrangements.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="fish-faq-item">
        <summary><h3>Can these FISH probes be used to identify chromosomes in other grass species?</h3></summary>
        <div class="fish-faq-body">
          <p>
            Several probes in this cocktail \(such as 5S rDNA, NOR, and telomeric sequences\) are conserved across grasses. Centromeric and knob probes \(CentC, Knob180, TR-1\) are specific to *Zea* and closely related *Tripsacum* species.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="fish-faq-item">
        <summary><h3>Where can I find additional cytogenetic chromosome maps at MaizeGDB?</h3></summary>
        <div class="fish-faq-body">
          <p>
            Visit the <a href="/data_center/cytogenetic">Cytogenetic Data Center</a> to search cytogenetic translocation stocks, deficiency mapping data, inversion breakpoints, and classical chromosome maps.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

